Render a frame as soon as an application commits, instead of on the next beat of the frame rate.

A patch against gst-wayland-display (https://github.com/games-on-whales/gst-wayland-display,
MIT License), commit 1301f86. The lines of context are from that project.

Only for the measurements of step 3 of docs/m1-plan.md: it shows what a compositor gains that
does not wait for a fixed rhythm (docs/measurements/2026-11-16-step3-render-on-commit.md).
The host of step 4 does this in its own compositor.

What it changes: a frame the element has asked for waits, as before, until one frame interval
has passed since the last one. If an application commits while it waits, it is rendered at
once; if an application committed before it was asked for, it does not wait at all.
Applications are still told to draw their next frame once per frame interval.

Known limit: the timer still renders a frame when nothing was committed, so an application
that draws at the frame rate gets some frames rendered twice (114 frames per second at a
frame rate of 85).

Apply and build, next to the unpatched checkout:

    git worktree add ../repo-commit 1301f86
    cd ../repo-commit && git apply <this file>
    cargo build --release -p gst-plugin-wayland-display -p wayland-display-core --features cuda

